  "content": "Formula: Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic\nExact Expression: n = \\prod_{i=1}^{k} p_i^{a_i}\nDefinitions: Every integer greater than 1 can be represented uniquely as a product of prime powers up to order of factors.\nDerivation/Reference: Euclid's Elements Book VII, Propositions 30-32; Carl Friedrich Gauss, Disquisitiones Arithmeticae (1801)\nExample: 360 = 2^3 * 3^2 * 5^1\nDomain: arithmetic\nAssumptions: n in Z, n > 1\nCommon Errors: Assuming 1 is prime (violates uniqueness)",
